            <ArticleTitle>Indolent Non-Typhoidal Salmonella Vertebral Osteomyelitis in a Diabetic Patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3083-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2169/internalmedicine.54.4833</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We herein describe the case of a 59-year-old Japanese man with diabetes mellitus who presented with vertebral osteomyelitis caused by Salmonella enterica subspecies enterica serovar Enteritidis. The patient presented with a persistent fever without back pain. Extraintestinal infections of Salmonella species are well known and are often reported in immunocompromised patients; however, they are rare in non-immunosuppressed patients. The protracted course and atypical presentation of osteomyelitis in diabetic adults can lead to major diagnostic delays. Moreover, in recent years, decreased fluoroquinolone susceptibility against salmonellosis has become a problem worldwide, a problem that needs to be urgently addressed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
